prompt:
{
  "public_instructions": "You translate natural-language access-control requirements into Cedar policies using the supplied schema. Cedar denies by default. An applicable forbid overrides every permit. Guard optional attributes with has before reading them. Preserve the schema's entity, set, and attribute types. Use only the supplied task information. Do not use tools, files, search, additional model calls, or external feedback. Produce a single final answer as a JSON object with exactly one field, policy, whose value is the complete Cedar policy text. Do not include explanations or Markdown fences.
",
  "harness": {
    "instruction_blocks": [
      "Translate the access-control requirements into a complete Cedar policy set. Identify each action, eligible principal, resource type, required condition, prohibition, and exception. Use only entity types, attributes, actions, and representations supported by the schema. Implement the stated permissions and restrictions together.",
      "Before answering, check the policy mentally for both unauthorized permissions and missing required permissions. Keep distinct action rules separate when their conditions differ. Apply exceptions only to the restriction they qualify. Return one final policy set."
    ],
    "input_order": [
      "requirements",
      "schema"
    ],
    "checklist": [
      "Every required action is covered.",
      "Role, ownership, membership, and context conditions follow the requirements.",
      "Prohibitions and exceptions compose correctly.",
      "Optional attribute reads are guarded and Cedar syntax is valid."
    ]
  },
  "requirements": "The system manages telemetry access for an IoT and operational technology device fleet.

The people involved are operators, technicians, fleet admins, and service accounts. One person may hold both the operator and the technician role at the same time.

The resources are devices, telemetry, and commands. Every device records its owner, site group, firmware tags, maintenance mode, and status, and every telemetry record records its device and sensitivity.

Each requester carries their clearance and the groups they belong to.

An operator or fleet admin may read telemetry only when they belong under the device's site group.

An operator or fleet admin outside the device's site group must not read telemetry for that device.

An operator or fleet admin who does not own the device may read its telemetry only when their clearance is at least as high as the telemetry sensitivity.

An operator or fleet admin who owns the device may read its telemetry without meeting the telemetry sensitivity level, as long as they belong under the device's site group.

Neither an operator nor a fleet admin may read telemetry for a device outside their site group.

A request by an operator to read telemetry that does not trip any prohibition above must be able to succeed.",
  "schema": "entity Role enum ["Operator", "Technician", "FleetAdmin", "ServiceAccount"];

entity SiteGroup in [SiteGroup];

entity Requester in [SiteGroup] {
    roles: Set<Role>,
    clearance: Long,
    groups: Set<SiteGroup>,
};

entity Device {
    owner: Requester,
    siteGroup: SiteGroup,
    firmwareTags: Set<String>,
    maintenanceMode: Bool,
    status: String,
};

entity TelemetryRecord {
    device: Device,
    sensitivity: Long,
};

entity Command;

action readTelemetry appliesTo {
    principal: [Requester],
    resource: [TelemetryRecord],
};
"
}
